#: ../../../onboarding/flask.rst:114
msgid "Sync and async handlers"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/flask.rst:116
msgid ""
"Both Flask and Litestar support synchronous and asynchronous functions, but "
"they are handled differently. Flask implements the `WSGI <https://peps."
"python.org/pep-3333/>`_ protocol, which is synchronous by nature, while "
"Litestar implements `ASGI <https://asgi.readthedocs.io>`_, an asynchronous "
"protocol."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/flask.rst:121
msgid ""
"When Flask runs asynchronous endpoints, they run isolated from another. The "
"handling of requests is still synchronous. Litestar is asynchronous "
"throughout, meaning one worker can handle many asynchronous requests "
"concurrently."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/flask.rst:125
msgid ""
"Litestar can also handle synchronous endpoints without blocking, by running "
"them in a thread pool. To enable this, set ``sync_to_thread=True`` on the "
"handler decorator."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/flask.rst:129
msgid ""
"A sync handler without ``sync_to_thread`` set emits a runtime warning, since "
"Litestar cannot tell whether the function blocks. Pass "
"``sync_to_thread=False`` to suppress the warning when the body genuinely "
"does not block."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/flask.rst:139
msgid ""
"@app.get(\"/\")\n"
"def slow_handler():\n"
"    # blocking I/O on the worker\n"
"    time.sleep(1)\n"
"    return {\"hello\": \"world\"}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/flask.rst:150
msgid ""
"from litestar import get\n"
"\n"
"@get(\"/\", sync_to_thread=True)\n"
"def slow_handler() -> dict[str, str]:\n"
"    # blocking on a thread, event loop remains free\n"
"    time.sleep(1)\n"
"    return {\"hello\": \"world\"}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/flask.rst:161 ../../../onboarding/flask.rst:854
msgid "Lifespan"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/flask.rst:163
msgid ""
"Flask has ``@before_first_request`` for startup work and no matching hook "
"for shutdown. Litestar accepts one or more async context managers through :"
"paramref:`~litestar.app.Litestar.lifespan`; setup goes before the ``yield``, "
"teardown after."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/flask.rst:169
msgid ""
"from collections.abc import AsyncIterator\n"
"from contextlib import asynccontextmanager\n"
"\n"
"from litestar import Litestar, get\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"@asynccontextmanager\n"
"async def lifespan(app: Litestar) -> AsyncIterator[None]:\n"
"    # Setup: open database pool, warm caches, etc.\n"
"    yield\n"
"    # Teardown: close resources.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"@get(\"/\")\n"
"async def index() -> dict[str, str]:\n"
"    return {\"hello\": \"world\"}\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"app = Litestar(route_handlers=[index], lifespan=[lifespan])"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/flask.rst:173
msgid "Accessing the request"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/flask.rst:175
msgid ""
"Flask exposes the current request through ``flask.request``, a thread-local "
"proxy. In Litestar a handler that needs the request asks for it by name; the "
"parameter is typed as :class:`~litestar.connection.Request`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/flask.rst:185
msgid ""
"from flask import Flask, request\n"
"\n"
"app = Flask(__name__)\n"
"\n"
"@app.get(\"/\")\n"
"def index():\n"
"    return {\"method\": request.method}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/flask.rst:198
msgid ""
"from litestar import Litestar, Request, get\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"@get(\"/\", sync_to_thread=False)\n"
"def index(request: Request) -> dict[str, str]:\n"
"    return {\"method\": request.method}\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"app = Litestar(route_handlers=[index])"
msgstr ""

